#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["textual>=8"]
# ///
"""Terminal dashboard for Dell PowerEdge / iDRAC fan control.

Shows live temperatures and fan RPMs, and lets you take the fans over by hand:

  AUTO    Daemon control  send nothing; the fan control daemon stays in charge
          BMC control     force the BMC's own automatic curve
  Manual                  pin all fans to a duty you choose with the slider

Moving the slider or pressing a duty button switches to Manual by itself, and going
back to Daemon control puts the slider back on the daemon's hold speed. You can also
start, stop or restart the fan control daemon service from here, and save the slider's
value as the daemon's hold speed (FAN_SPEED in the config; applied on its next restart).

While you are in BMC control or Manual this writes its PID to the override file, so
ipmi-fancontrol-daemon stands down instead of fighting you. If this program dies,
the PID goes stale and the daemon resumes by itself within one poll.

Manual mode has no thermal response. It drops back to BMC control if a CPU reaches
CPU_TEMP_THRESHOLD or the temperatures cannot be read, on quit, and in a last-resort
exit handler if the program crashes. (If the daemon is stopped and the program is
killed outright with SIGKILL, nothing can restore the fans.)

Reads conf/ipmi-fancontrol.conf like the shell scripts do. Needs root for the local
"open" interface. Run it directly (uv fetches Textual on first use):

    ./ipmi-fancontrol-tui           the dashboard
    ./ipmi-fancontrol-tui --check   print one reading and exit; changes nothing
"""

from __future__ import annotations

